  ib betoni laatu get 42 --asiakas 8
"
`;

exports[`Help snapshots — contract stability > help for ib betoni laatu list matches snapshot 1`] = `
"USAGE
  ib betoni laatu list [flags]

DESCRIPTION
  List the concrete grades one supplier can offer (GET /api/betoni/laatu/list/:betoniToimittajaAsiakasId): its OWN rows plus the shared (yhteinen) ones, in sortNum order. Each row carries a derived \`shared\` boolean so the two populations the response mixes can be told apart.
  Auth: requires login (any authenticated user).

FLAGS
  --asiakas NUMBER    Supplier (betoniToimittajaAsiakasId) whose catalogue to read; default = your active company
  --search STRING    Client-side substring filter over laatuNimike / laatuLyhenne / laatuSelite
  --shared-only    Only the shared (asiakasId 0) grades
  --own-only    Only the supplier's own grades (excludes the shared ones)

GLOBAL FLAGS
  --endpoint URL  --request-id ID  --quiet  --verbose  --pretty  --json  --read-only  --company ID  --stats  --columns CSV  --print-payload
  --company runs ONE command as another company (ephemeral, not persisted) · ib help multi-tenancy

OUTPUT (JSON, stdout)
  ListEnvelope<{ laatuId, laatuNimike, laatuLyhenne, laatuLaji, laatuSelite, sortNum, asiakasId, shared, isEnabled, showInDropDown, laatuAllowedS, laatuAllowedRae, laatuAllowedC, laatuShortCuts, laatuHelpId }>

ERRORS (stderr, exit non-zero)
  exit 4 (client-side)  --shared-only and --own-only both given → they name two disjoint sets — pass one, or neither for both
  exit 4 (client-side)  no usable ownerAsiakasId on the token → pass --asiakas <supplierId>, or \`ib auth switch\`
  exit 4 (HTTP 400)  Invalid betoniToimittajaAsiakasId → --asiakas must be a non-negative integer
  exit 2 (HTTP 401)  Token expired          → ib auth refresh

NOTES
  - asiakasId 0 is the SHARED (yhteinen) grade pool visible to every tenant; anything else is that supplier's own. The backend returns both in one list with no marker — \`shared\` is derived client-side.
  - Deliberately NOT restricted to your own tenant: a customer legitimately reads its SUPPLIER's catalogue, which is why the backend scopes the cache key by supplier rather than by caller.
  - The rows come from betoniLaatuView. laatuAllowedRae/laatuAllowedS/laatuAllowedC are expressed in the vocabularies \`ib betoni reference\` returns.

SEE ALSO
  ib betoni laatu get, ib betoni reference

EXAMPLES
  ib betoni laatu list
  ib betoni laatu list --asiakas 8 --shared-only
  ib betoni laatu list --search rapid --pretty
"
`;

exports[`Help snapshots — contract stability > help for ib betoni reference matches snapshot 1`] = `
"USAGE
  ib betoni reference [flags]

DESCRIPTION
  The four fixed concrete lookup lists — raekoko (aggregate size), lujuus (strength), notkeus (consistency), kayttoika (working life) — in ONE call. These are the vocabularies a grade's laatuAllowedRae / laatuAllowedS / laatuAllowedC fields are expressed in.
  Auth: none (public).

FLAGS
  --kind STRING    Return only one list

GLOBAL FLAGS
  --endpoint URL  --request-id ID  --quiet  --verbose  --pretty  --json  --read-only  --company ID  --stats  --columns CSV  --print-payload
  --company runs ONE command as another company (ephemeral, not persisted) · ib help multi-tenancy

OUTPUT (JSON, stdout)
  { raekoko: [...], lujuus: [...], notkeus: [...], kayttoika: [...] } — narrowed to the single key when --kind is given

ERRORS (stderr, exit non-zero)
  exit 4 (client-side)  unknown --kind value   → one of: raekoko, lujuus, notkeus, kayttoika
  exit 6 (HTTP 500)  Backend error          → retry with --verbose

NOTES
  - Bundled rather than split into four leaves because they are read together: decoding one grade's allowed-values fields needs all four vocabularies at once.
  - These four routes are unauthenticated reference data, cached server-side with a 2-hour TTL.

SEE ALSO
  ib betoni laatu list

EXAMPLES
  ib betoni reference
  ib betoni reference --kind raekoko
"
`;

exports[`Help snapshots — contract stability > help for ib commands matches snapshot 1`] = `
"USAGE
  ib commands [<domain>] [flags]

DESCRIPTION
  Offline command discovery from the spec catalogue. No args = compact DOMAIN INDEX (~5 KB: every domain with leaf count, glossary blurb, runnable command paths). A domain arg, a filter flag, or --all returns the flat per-command list { command, description, permissions, isWrite }. Lighter than \`ib reference dump\` (the full surface). No auth, no network.
  Auth: none (public).

ARGUMENTS
  <domain> STRING    Only commands in this domain (the token after \`ib\`, e.g. keikka, jerry). Unknown domain exits 4 listing valid domains. (optional)

FLAGS
  --mutations    Only commands that write (carry write-safety flags)
  --reads    Only read-only commands (no writes)
  --permission STRING    Only commands whose required permissions contain this substring
  --find STRING    Keyword search: only commands whose PATH, description, or flag names contain this case-insensitive substring. Intent-first discovery when you know the concept but not the domain (driver lives under vehicle, geocode under sijainti) — offline, composes AND-wise with the domain arg and the other filters, returns the flat list. No match = empty list (exit 0); empty text exits 4.
  --all    Full flat list of every command (~43 KB at 149 leaves). Default (no args) is the domain index.
  --signatures    Add each command's compact call shape to the rows: \`args\` (\`<name:type>\` required, \`[name:type]\` optional) and \`flags\` signature strings (\`--f <type>\`; \`<a|b>\` allowed values; \`!\` required, \`*\` one-of-a-required-group). The middle rung between the flat list and \`ib reference dump\` — everything needed to CONSTRUCT most calls at a fraction of the dump's tokens; the envelope's leading \`hint\` spells out the notation and the write-safety trio. Triggers the flat list on its own; composes with the domain arg and every filter.

GLOBAL FLAGS
  --endpoint URL  --request-id ID  --quiet  --verbose  --pretty  --json  --read-only  --company ID  --stats  --columns CSV  --print-payload
  --company runs ONE command as another company (ephemeral, not persisted) · ib help multi-tenancy

OUTPUT (JSON, stdout)
  no args: { hint, items:[{ domain, count, description|null, commands:["keikka list", ...] }], nextCursor:null, count } (domain index) | with <domain> / --all / filters: { items: [{ command, description, permissions: string[], isWrite: boolean }], nextCursor: null, count } | --signatures adds per-row args?: string[] + flags?: string[] call-shape signatures and a leading envelope \`hint\` explaining the notation

ERRORS (stderr, exit non-zero)
  exit 4 (client-side)  Bad flag combo         → --mutations and --reads are mutually exclusive
  exit 4 (client-side)  Unknown domain         → run \`ib commands\` (no arg) to see valid domains
  exit 4 (client-side)  --find given empty/whitespace text (a PowerShell bare "" argument is dropped and swallows the next flag) → pass real search text: ib commands --find geocode

EXAMPLES
  ib commands
  ib commands keikka
  ib commands --all
  ib commands --find geocode
  ib commands vehicle --find driver --reads
  ib commands keikka --signatures
  ib commands --all --signatures
  ib commands --mutations
  ib commands --permission auth.page.vehicle
  ib commands --mutations | jq '.items[].command'
"
`;

